data one_dissolution_event_recorded_twice_or_not_at_all: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "one_dissolution_event_recorded_twice_or_not_at_all" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**one dissolution event, recorded twice or not at all** (a roster rule assigns deletion of CONSUMED admissions to the next roster-touching change. ",

    "The rule is CORRECT AND THIS ROW DOES NOT DISPUTE IT: the obligation reaches every concurrent roster-touching change and is discharged by whichever observes it first, exactly as its own prose says. ",

    "What recurs is not a broken trigger but an ASYMMETRY BETWEEN THE TWO THINGS IT OBLIGES, AND BOTH HALVES OF IT CAN MERGE SILENTLY. ",

    "The DELETION COMPOSES IDEMPOTENTLY: two branches removing the same rows compose to one absence with no conflict, which the authority itself records -- `v1_compiler.namespace_wave_admission` says of the 57-row case MAIN EMPTIED INDEPENDENTLY, WHICH IS THE TRIGGER MECHANISM WORKING RATHER THAN A COLLISION. ",

    "The RECORD COMPOSES ADDITIVELY: each observer authors an ordinal-bearing dissolution writeup, and two writeups of ONE event merge cleanly into two. ",

    "AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS ROW CLAIMED THE DELETION COLLIDES LOUDLY, WHICH ITS OWN CITED SPECIMEN CONTRADICTS, and the error is instructive: this row's author generalised from a TEXTUAL CONFLICT they hit on gunbc#10248, which was an ADD/ADD on a roster line and not a deletion at all. ",

    "Idempotence is exactly what lets the duplicated observation collapse while the duplicated history survives, so the deletion never reliably announces the race. ",

    "INVALID STATE: one dissolution event carrying two records, or carrying none. ",

    "HARM: both wrong arms are silent and neither is countable. ",

    "Yield when nothing else is recording and N admissions vanish from main unexplained, which is precisely what the roster history exists to prevent; author when something else is recording and one event acquires two ordinal-bearing records, a DESIGN section 3 meaning fork committed inside the ledger that explains the deletion. ",

    "THE DECIDING INPUT IS UNREADABLE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CLASS: choosing between the arms requires knowing whether a competing branch is LIVE AND RECORDING. ",

    "That is not derivable from the artifact, is not visible to review, and CHANGES BETWEEN CI ROUNDS -- so the decision is correct or incorrect for reasons the author cannot read and a later reader cannot reconstruct. ",

    "No amount of care closes it. ",

    "DISCOVERED BY valiant-bee-316 AND tidy-koi-619 across their own cuts; drafted here by a third lane at their request, from shas they supplied, so that one record exists rather than two -- which is this row obeying its own subject. ",

    "The two main-side quotations below were verified verbatim against origin/main by the drafter rather than relayed. ",

    "SPECIMENS -- THREE EVENTS, FIVE INSTANCES, TWO BRANCHES, ABOUT TWENTY HOURS. ",

    "EVENT 1, 57 rows: main and a branch deleted the same consumed rows independently, recorded in main as SEVENTEENTH TRANSITION in `v1_compiler.namespace_wave_admission` -- neither aware of the other, both paying the rule. ",

    "No double record, because one side was main. ",

    "EVENT 2, 3 rows, consumed when gunbc#10011 merged as 4acf8ac234: TWO branches observed it independently and BOTH authored NINETEENTH DISSOLUTION -- 889a5f52493 and 29db34e975. ",

    "One was yielded at 2fafa0f9591, deletion kept and record dropped in favour of a pointer. ",

    "THIS IS THE CLASS FIRING AND BEING CAUGHT BY HAND. ",

    "EVENT 3, 47 rows, consumed when gunbc#10106 merged as 78e022c51e4: only one branch observed it, so the same trigger correctly produced the OPPOSITE action -- record rather than yield, TWENTIETH DISSOLUTION at edbfcd7b45f. ",

    "SAME TRIGGER, OPPOSITE CORRECT ACTION, DECIDED ENTIRELY BY WHETHER A COMPETING RECORD EXISTED. ",

    "Plus three ORDINAL COLLISIONS on one file from one branch: SIXTEENTH TRANSITION taken mid-branch by gunbc#10010, then SEVENTEENTH TRANSITION and NINETEENTH DISSOLUTION both taken by gunbc#10106, forcing EIGHTEENTH TRANSITION -- the same silent-merge surface reached through the ordinal rather than the record. ",

    "THE COST AND THE MECHANISM ARE TWO CLAIMS AND A READER WHO CONFLATES THEM REPAIRS THE WRONG THING. ",

    "The cost is real: a branch touching the roster for its own unrelated reasons inherits another lane's sweep, each inheritance costing a commit, a CI round and a judgement call -- one call-reachability cut paid it TWICE for two upstream transitions it did not cause. ",

    "But the same evidence shows the TRIGGER WORKING. ",

    "Repairing the trigger would break the only part that is sound. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable, resting on author diligence -- nothing refuses either wrong arm and nothing counts them. ",

    "CEILING: 2, mechanically preventable, and the reason it is not higher is that the record is author-assigned PROSE; making the state unwritable means the record ceasing to be prose, which is a carrier question this row deliberately does not decide. ",

    "Reaching 2 is decidable, BUT NOT FROM THE MERGE-BASE ALONE: a base containing no record admits BOTH concurrent branches, each locally clean, and the duplicate prose then composes without conflict -- so a check with perfect merge-base access still passes both and admits the second record. ",

    "Decidability requires the EXACT WOULD-LAND COMPOSITION at a serialized boundary, which is why the trigger below names that rather than the base. ",

    "NOT MEASURED, AND CARRIED AS UNMEASURED BECAUSE THE ORIGINATING LANE FLAGGED IT: whether the merge-base is REACHABLE to such a check at the point the admission phase runs. ",

    "If it is not, the ceiling argument needs re-deriving before anyone builds against it. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as the CAPABILITY AND JOINING BOTH DIRECTIONS: at the serialized merge boundary, derive a dissolution-event identity from the CONSUMED ADMISSION POPULATION and require the exact would-land tree to contain EXACTLY ONE history record per event and EXACTLY ONE event per record -- refusing zero, refusing duplicates, and refusing or re-executing when current main differs from the main revision adjudicated. ",

    "AN AT-MOST-ONE WALL IS NOT SUFFICIENT AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS ROW NAMED ONE: refusing a record whose label already carries one says nothing about consumed rows deleted with NO record, which is half of the invalid state above. ",

    "That is the section 4b(3) grain mismatch -- a two-armed loss under a one-armed trigger -- committed in the same row that warns against it. ",

    "ITS DISCRIMINATING REDS ARE BOTH ARMS AND ONE OF THEM MUST CROSS THE RACE: consumed rows deleted with ZERO record; one event carrying TWO records; and, because a static two-record fixture does not exercise the actual specimen, TWO CONCURRENT BRANCHES SHARING A BASE WITH NO RECORD, each locally clean and admissible, where the SECOND composition refuses after the first lands. ",

    "Positive control: one event, one record, admitted. ",

    "CROSS-REFERENCE, NOT A MERGER: `ambient_process_state_read_by_a_concurrent_reader` shares the sentence CORRECTNESS DEPENDS ON CONCURRENT STATE THE ACTOR CANNOT READ, but there the defect is a READ that returns a well-formed value about a different subject, repaired by making the ambient fact a parameter. ",

    "Here it is a WRITE DECISION with no readable input at all, repaired by making a second record refuse. ",

    "Neither repair transfers, which is DESIGN section 4b's keying-on-the-repair test.)",
